(*
expect:
health 61
metrics 101
cheap 62
batch 385
*)
(* #56 design §9.1, landed as a REGRESSION GUARD.

   *** THE FRAME FITS AGAIN AS OF rete#197 (2026-08-11): 385 B against the 512 B
   *** cap, 127 B of headroom, and 378 B once the envelope's own two labels
   *** shorten. It was 529 B — SEVENTEEN OVER — for one day, and the gauge went
   *** quiet on a live node for the whole of it (see below).

   TWO WAYS THIS GUARD WAS MEASURING A FRAME THE NODE NO LONGER SENDS, both found
   and fixed 2026-08-11 (rete#190 stage 2 review):

   1. It PRINTED `cheap 79` but never added the row. rete#190 made `publishCheap`
      write `sys/cheap` on every check-in beside `sys/metrics`, so the steady-state
      frame is FOUR rows; this file went on measuring three. A guard edited into
      agreement with the code, against its own header, guards nothing — the whole
      point of the 2026-08-09 note below is that a field can eat the headroom
      silently, and that is exactly what happened.
   2. The ENVELOPE's `bootId` was still the constant 1. The 2026-08-09 note claimed
      the saturated-counter cost was "paid once per batch envelope and not per row",
      but the value it actually changed was the one inside the Health record. The
      envelope carries its own `bootId` and it was never widened, so the guard was
      4 B light on top of everything else. It is 1073741823 here now, for the reason
      the Health field gives: a guard must prove the frame fits for ANY legal value.

   Was 425/87 B, then 429/83 B until 2026-08-09. The honest series is
   425 -> 429 (health bootId widened) -> 433 (envelope bootId widened, the 4 B the
   note above thought it had already counted) -> 529 (the sys/cheap row, 96 B: a
   79 B record plus 17 B of seq, path and kind) -> 385 (rete#197, every record's
   labels shortened) -> 378 (rete#197, the envelope's two as well).

   WHAT THE OVERFLOW COST, WITNESSED AND NOT MODELLED. This guard went red on
   2026-08-11 as a prediction about long-uptime nodes. Within the day it was
   confirmed in the field: `sys/cheap` stopped reaching porta from `c6-bench`
   entirely — last delivered seq 70006 against a live 78839, a gap of ~8 800 —
   while `obs/health`, `sys/metrics` and `tel/log` kept arriving every 2 s.
   `publishCheap` writes the cell on EVERY check-in (kernel.nsl), so the node was
   producing it and the frame was dropping it; earlier in the same window it was
   arriving at 4-6 s gaps, i.e. trimmed on some check-ins and then on all of them
   as the counters widened. The gauge went quiet on exactly the node it exists to
   watch, which is what this file said would happen.

   WHY ALL THE LABELS AND NOT JUST `CHeap`'s. Shortening the five `CHeap` field
   names was the first proposal, and it was MEASURED NOT TO REACH — one-character
   names, the floor where names stop meaning anything, land the frame at 503 B, and
   any legible scheme (`out`/`high`/`na`/`nf`/`fails`) lands ~514 and still
   overflows. That measurement is what forced one rule across every label rather
   than a local patch. Numbered slots (`h1`, `m1`, ...) save 23 B more and were
   rejected: they make meaning POSITIONAL, so a mismatched reader decodes `m7`
   cleanly and silently means something else, where an unknown NAME is loud.

   WHAT THIS DID NOT FIX. rete#150 is still open and still unfunded. This bought
   127 B of headroom; it did not change the trim rule, which still takes the TAIL
   and therefore still drops the freshest, highest-seq row first. The structural
   answer `PortaBridge>>pushFrame` parks — reserve bytes for `sys/` before filling
   from `obs/`, or trim BY TYPE — is unchanged, and it cannot be done by reordering,
   because porta acks ONE high-water and `applyAck:` sets `acked:: hw`: a frame that
   omits a low seq while carrying a high one loses the omitted entry permanently,
   which is precisely why the trim takes the tail.

   These are THIRTY-DAY values, and that is the whole point of the fixture: at bench
   uptimes the frame fitted even at 529 B, and the host check-in path delivered 143
   `sys/cheap` rows in 150 ticks (design §11.7) while a real node three weeks into
   its uptime delivered none.

   NOT a sum of record sizes: the thing that must fit 512 B is the PUSH FRAME —
   envelope and rows included — so this builds the frame PortaBridge>>encodePush:
   builds and encodes it through the real Cbor path.

   Values are for a node that has been up THIRTY DAYS, because CBOR is shortest-form
   and a fresh node encodes narrower than a long-lived one: a field holding 1296000
   costs five bytes where one holding 12 costs one. Measuring the easy case is exactly
   how an estimate becomes wrong in the field.

   `metrics 101` is the CONTROL. It is the value RECORDS §4.4 pins through a
   different test (19_latency/05_record_size), so if it moves, the failure is not in
   this file and every other number here is suspect. It was 195 B under the long
   labels, pinned 2026-07-21; both tests moved to 101 together on 2026-08-11, which
   is what keeps the control a control.

   Note: `platform Cbor` answers an INSTANCE where `platform Spans` answers the CLASS
   — an asymmetry worth knowing before copying either line. *)
class BatchSize packageUsing: manifest = () (

  (* obs/health as design D2 slimmed it: liveness and delivery, five fields. *)
  health: platform = (
    | pairs |
    pairs:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    pairs add: #up -> 2592000000.      (* 30 days *)
    (* rete#162: the WORST LEGAL bootId, not a fresh node's. This was 1 while
       bootId was a source constant; it is now a counter persisted in NVS across
       the node's whole lifetime, and CBOR is shortest-form, so the value decides
       the field width — 1 costs one byte and a saturated counter costs five. A
       guard must prove the frame fits for ANY legal value, and 2^30-1 is the
       band's ceiling (the VM keeps it inside an SMI on a 32-bit target). This is
       the same reasoning as the 30-day uptime beside it: measuring the easy case
       is exactly how an estimate becomes wrong in the field. *)
    pairs add: #bid -> 1073741823.
    pairs add: #wak -> 1296000.            (* 30 d at the 2 s cadence *)
    pairs add: #pto -> 431.
    pairs add: #sdr -> 17.
    ^ platform Record tag: #Health fields: (platform Map fromPairs: pairs) )

  (* The eleven fields kernel.nsl builds, with the WROOM-32 values 05_record_size.nsl
     measured, so the 195 B control reproduces exactly. *)
  metrics: platform = (
    | pairs |
    pairs:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    pairs add: #hsz -> 53248.
    pairs add: #lb -> 36196.
    pairs add: #hhw -> 53228.
    pairs add: #lfb -> 18608.
    pairs add: #gcc -> 1523.
    pairs add: #hnl -> 1.
    pairs add: #hnhw -> 1.
    pairs add: #qhw -> 2.
    pairs add: #ovr -> 0.
    pairs add: #pdr -> 0.
    pairs add: #pco -> 0.
    ^ platform Record tag: #Metrics fields: (platform Map fromPairs: pairs) )

  (* rete#190: the five CHeapStats fields, in kernel.nsl's field order. Counts are
     a 30-day node's plausible worst case (frequent alloc/free churn, matching the
     `wakes` magnitude above), not a boot-time snapshot — the health/bootId
     precedent above is why: measuring the easy case is how an estimate goes
     wrong in the field.
     outBytes/outHigh are bounded by the C heap itself, ~200 KB on the C6
     (platform.c:1262's own comment on PRIM_pf_cheapStats), NOT by the small
     boot-time footprint the invariants test exercises — that bound crosses
     CBOR's 65536 boundary into the 5-byte uint class, same as bootId crossing
     it above. 131072/196608 (128 KiB / 192 KiB) sit under the ~200 KB ceiling
     with outHigh > outBytes, as the invariant requires. *)
  cheap: platform = (
    | pairs |
    pairs:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    pairs add: #ob -> 131072.
    pairs add: #ohw -> 196608.
    pairs add: #alc -> 1296000.
    pairs add: #fre -> 1295998.
    pairs add: #fal -> 2.
    ^ platform Record tag: #CHeap fields: (platform Map fromPairs: pairs) )

  log: platform = (
    ^ platform Record tag: #Log with: #lvl -> #info
                              with: #txt -> 'tick 1296000' )

  row: platform seq: s path: p value: v = (
    ^ platform List with: s with: p with: 2 with: v )

public main: platform args: args = (
  | cbor rows |
  cbor:: platform Cbor.
  ('health ' , (cbor encode: (self health: platform)) byteSize printString) println.
  ('metrics ' , (cbor encode: (self metrics: platform)) byteSize printString) println.
  ('cheap ' , (cbor encode: (self cheap: platform)) byteSize printString) println.

  rows:: platform OrderedCollection new.
  rows add: (self row: platform seq: 1296000 path: 'obs/health' value: (self health: platform)).
  rows add: (self row: platform seq: 1296001 path: 'sys/metrics' value: (self metrics: platform)).
  rows add: (self row: platform seq: 1296002 path: 'tel/log' value: (self log: platform)).
  (* rete#190: publishCheap writes this every check-in, immediately after
     sys/metrics — so it carries the HIGHEST seq of the four and is the first row
     pushFrame's tail-trim drops. It belongs in the frame the guard measures. *)
  rows add: (self row: platform seq: 1296003 path: 'sys/cheap' value: (self cheap: platform)).
  ('batch ' , (cbor encode: (platform Record tag: #push
      with: #id -> 'c6-bench'
      (* The ENVELOPE's bootId, which is a different field from Health's and was
         left at 1 until 2026-08-11. Worst legal value, same reasoning. *)
      with: #bootId -> 1073741823
      with: #entries -> (platform List fromCollection: rows))) byteSize printString) println.
)
) : (
)
